WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:03.330
In this section for your stations,

2
00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.570
we're going to demo the LED panel replacement

3
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:08.970
that you're going to be doing on your stage location.

4
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.750
At the Lantai stage, we're using the LED panel hardware

5
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.990
for the ROH Black Pearl II panels,

6
00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.689
and things that you should note

7
00:00:15.689 --> 00:00:17.289
is that there should be a backup panel

8
00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:19.250
available for replacement modules.

9
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.410
Now each panel has four labeled modules,

10
00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:23.089
and we're gonna show those in just a moment,

11
00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:25.230
but they have a left top, left bottom,

12
00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.050
right top, and right bottom,

13
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.850
and you'll only need to replace the module

14
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.609
with the same module location.

15
00:00:30.609 --> 00:00:32.649
For example, left top to left top,

16
00:00:32.649 --> 00:00:34.770
which is what we're going to be doing today.

17
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.689
Now as I walk through these steps,

18
00:00:36.689 --> 00:00:38.789
you are going to be the one that does this

19
00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:41.689
if you don't have a larger technician

20
00:00:41.689 --> 00:00:44.469
that is actually onsite to manage your stage

21
00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:45.929
and your LED panels.

22
00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:48.409
For smaller steps, you may run into this.

23
00:00:48.409 --> 00:00:50.170
Now it is recommended that when you do this,

24
00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.450
you do have a second person,

25
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.570
and Sean Spitzer is going to join me while we do this,

26
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.490
and that will help ensure that you don't

27
00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.909
accidentally drop or damage the panel during removal.

28
00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:01.750
Now, as I showed you the other steps,

29
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.109
you can go back and view that to pause,

30
00:01:04.109 --> 00:01:05.469
and then you wanna finish this

31
00:01:05.469 --> 00:01:07.670
with these final steps to actually replace this.

32
00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.030
Now we'll walk through these,

33
00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.930
and occasionally I do wanna note,

34
00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:11.989
and I will mention this again,

35
00:01:11.989 --> 00:01:14.250
that the data cable will be disconnected

36
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.510
if it doesn't automatically re-sync.

37
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.450
Now let's switch over and take a look

38
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.269
at the hardware we're going to be working with today.

39
00:01:21.269 --> 00:01:25.170
Now you'll see before me is a Black Pearl II from Rho,

40
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.930
and inside the panel I've already removed

41
00:01:27.969 --> 00:01:30.250
the panel that we're going to be working with today.

42
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.409
So if I look at this panel,

43
00:01:31.409 --> 00:01:32.890
you'll see that there are four quadrants,

44
00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.569
and I've actually removed the left top quadrant

45
00:01:35.569 --> 00:01:37.170
in preparation for the panel

46
00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.129
that we're actually going to replace.

47
00:01:39.129 --> 00:01:43.310
The back of this panel has several sections

48
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.150
that are in here,

49
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.269
and you can see the brain for the processor

50
00:01:48.269 --> 00:01:50.030
and each of the individual panel units

51
00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.709
that do connect in here.

52
00:01:51.709 --> 00:01:54.269
Now additionally, I do recommend that you wanna make sure

53
00:01:54.269 --> 00:01:57.090
that you have on hand a Phillips head screwdriver

54
00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.829
or whichever one that is actually going to fit

55
00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:02.230
within the screws that are inside your panel.

56
00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:03.829
Now, as I said before,

57
00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:06.590
we have already set aside this panel

58
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.189
in order to be worked with,

59
00:02:08.189 --> 00:02:10.389
and this individual panel right here

60
00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:12.530
is one of the four sections.

61
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:13.949
One of the things I wanna note here

62
00:02:13.949 --> 00:02:15.789
is that inside each panel,

63
00:02:15.789 --> 00:02:18.319
you'll see that there is a notation

64
00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:21.229
for which panel it is actually located,

65
00:02:21.229 --> 00:02:24.370
and the actual connector bracket right here.

66
00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:25.870
We'll see those again up close

67
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.090
as we do the actual switch.

68
00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.009
Okay, so now let's take a look at the panel

69
00:02:30.009 --> 00:02:31.669
that we are ready here to replace.

70
00:02:31.669 --> 00:02:33.530
Now you'll note I've already marked this panel

71
00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:34.990
with some tape in order to note

72
00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.830
that the panel itself is bad.

73
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.490
Now what constitutes a bad panel here

74
00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.610
because it actually looks like it's going okay?

75
00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.650
If you wanna take a closer look here,

76
00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.849
this panel actually has some broken and dead pixels

77
00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:47.789
right along this edge.

78
00:02:47.789 --> 00:02:50.129
So you can see that there's some damaged pixels here

79
00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:52.360
and here.

80
00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.300
Now, if we move back behind this panel,

81
00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.240
this is where we begin our replacement,

82
00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.560
and you'll note here the corresponding number

83
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.439
and location information.

84
00:03:01.439 --> 00:03:03.879
This tells me which panel is here

85
00:03:03.879 --> 00:03:07.560
and the location, the left top located right there.

86
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.159
Now, as I wanna get ready and get started,

87
00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:10.680
you'll note that I've got a blue light here

88
00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:11.879
showing on my panel,

89
00:03:11.879 --> 00:03:15.870
and all my panels are connected top to bottom.

90
00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.189
Now to begin our panel replacement,

91
00:03:19.189 --> 00:03:21.669
the first thing I wanna do is click right here,

92
00:03:21.669 --> 00:03:24.539
move this out, and get that out of the way

93
00:03:24.539 --> 00:03:27.159
so that this locking mechanism is out of the way.

94
00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:30.280
Next, I'm gonna grab this silver connector,

95
00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.460
push that up, and twist to remove this.

96
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.199
And I'm just gonna hide this right here

97
00:03:35.199 --> 00:03:37.340
so it's out of the way while I work.

98
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.460
Now you'll notice these panels are now off,

99
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.319
and every panel below in the chain is off.

100
00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:46.360
But above this chain right here is still active.

101
00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.800
And that's because it feeds from top to bottom,

102
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.759
and each of these 10 panels, left to right,

103
00:03:51.759 --> 00:03:54.740
are connected in a single chain.

104
00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.180
Now, to unscrew them,

105
00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.060
I just go ahead and unscrew,

106
00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.460
and most of these are kept pretty finger loose

107
00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.939
so that I can actually get to them pretty easily.

108
00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:05.939
But if you need to make these adjustments here,

109
00:04:05.939 --> 00:04:09.919
you may need a screwdriver, as I mentioned before.

110
00:04:09.919 --> 00:04:11.960
Now, you'll also need to reach in

111
00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.360
and get these loose as well.

112
00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.240
But if you have an issue like me,

113
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:20.490
you'll wanna use the screwdriver.

114
00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.670
Now, I know that this is actually interconnected

115
00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.089
because if I push on this,

116
00:04:24.089 --> 00:04:27.050
you can see that this screw is bouncing.

117
00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:28.730
And that's because there's a magnet in here.

118
00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.449
So if I press here, you can see that it moves a little bit.

119
00:04:31.449 --> 00:04:32.810
Now, I'm ready to remove this.

120
00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.089
I just pull this forward

121
00:04:34.089 --> 00:04:35.970
so this handle is lined up straight,

122
00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.730
and I put two fingers in between this handle.

123
00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.649
And now, with ready and even pressure,

124
00:04:41.649 --> 00:04:44.579
I just push straight ahead.

125
00:04:44.579 --> 00:04:46.839
Now, since my hand has gone through this,

126
00:04:46.839 --> 00:04:50.730
I hand this to Sean, who then takes over.

127
00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:52.089
Now, as I mentioned before,

128
00:04:52.089 --> 00:04:54.209
I wanna make sure that these brackets are here,

129
00:04:54.209 --> 00:04:55.449
and you can see on this panel

130
00:04:55.449 --> 00:04:56.730
while I grab the other one,

131
00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.930
and Sean will show you on the actual brain

132
00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:01.019
where that connector's located.

133
00:05:01.019 --> 00:05:02.240
Now, you can see the pin here.

134
00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.740
You have to be very careful you get these in the right spot

135
00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.819
but it's pretty intuitive and easy to do

136
00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:08.379
so you don't have to hammer it in.

137
00:05:08.379 --> 00:05:10.040
So be very careful with that.

138
00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.660
But they do have magnets which help us

139
00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.180
with our location and putting things in place,

140
00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.139
which is fantastic for the process.

141
00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:18.480
So I'm ready again to replace my panel.

142
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.259
Now, my panel right now is upside down.

143
00:05:21.259 --> 00:05:22.300
So I'm gonna turn this.

144
00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.959
And the reason I know it's upside down

145
00:05:23.959 --> 00:05:25.259
is because as Sean mentioned,

146
00:05:25.259 --> 00:05:29.300
this connector here lines up with this connector here.

147
00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.420
Now, to get these lined up,

148
00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.060
I can go ahead and feed this to the bottom

149
00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:33.939
so that this is here.

150
00:05:33.939 --> 00:05:35.579
And there's little magnets here.

151
00:05:35.579 --> 00:05:37.420
You'll notice there are four magnets.

152
00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.339
This helps keep this in place.

153
00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:41.389
So I'm gonna just line this up,

154
00:05:41.389 --> 00:05:44.959
pop that in place, and it just fits nicely.

155
00:05:44.959 --> 00:05:46.000
Now, I'm gonna move back

156
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.620
to the other side of the panel again.

157
00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.029
And now I just need to return my screws.

158
00:05:53.029 --> 00:05:54.790
So, starting with the bottom,

159
00:05:54.790 --> 00:06:03.259
just line that up and screw that in.

160
00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:04.699
And again, I wanna keep it pretty loose

161
00:06:04.699 --> 00:06:07.040
just in case I need to replace it again.

162
00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.779
Same with the top.

163
00:06:08.779 --> 00:06:15.620
Get that in and screw that in

164
00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:18.259
so it's just tight enough to keep it in place.

165
00:06:18.259 --> 00:06:20.819
Now, I wanna replace this cable

166
00:06:20.819 --> 00:06:25.939
and I go ahead and feed this back in and twist.

167
00:06:25.939 --> 00:06:28.939
As that happens, light comes back on and it's red.

168
00:06:28.939 --> 00:06:31.060
The indicators below are also red.

169
00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.139
And in about 30 seconds,

170
00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:35.060
this will turn blue and the front will turn on.

171
00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.459
Slide that back in place

172
00:06:36.459 --> 00:06:39.290
and now let's watch as these turn back on.

173
00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:41.230
There will be times when you are putting

174
00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:43.230
your LED volume panels back in.

175
00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.470
They may need a little push or a little whack,

176
00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.449
but again, take that with a grain of salt.

177
00:06:47.449 --> 00:06:49.310
Every time is gonna be a little bit different,

178
00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:50.990
but just be careful when you do it

179
00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:54.050
and it should pop in just nicely.

180
00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.240
There we go.

181
00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.850
All set in place and back to normal.

182
00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.250
Okay, so if your panels don't actually connect

183
00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.329
and you don't see them all refreshing in the signal

184
00:07:04.329 --> 00:07:05.569
after you connect here,

185
00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:06.930
one of the things you might need to do

186
00:07:07.569 --> 00:07:09.600
is remove this network cable

187
00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.259
and reseed the network cable.

188
00:07:12.259 --> 00:07:14.579
Once that happens, they should pop back on again

189
00:07:14.579 --> 00:07:15.660
in 30 seconds.
